{"type":"exercise","type_name":"Exercise","number":"","title":"A1","title_html":"A1","html":"<div class=\"enumerate\" data-options=\"label=(\\alph*)\">\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>State <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"thm-simultaneous-unitary-diagonalization\">@thm-simultaneous-unitary-diagonalization</span>,\nnaming every hypothesis.</li>\n<li>Which result of Chapter 9 makes the eigenspaces of one member\ninvariant under the others, and which hypothesis does it use?</li>\n<li>Why is the restriction of a normal operator to an invariant subspace\nagain normal, and which earlier result says so? Why does the same fail\nfor a subspace that is not invariant?</li>\n<li>True or false: two commuting diagonalizable matrices are\nsimultaneously <strong>unitarily</strong> diagonalizable. Justify your\nanswer.</li>\n<li>True or false: if <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"S\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D446 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> and <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"T\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D447 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> are normal then <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"S + T\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D446 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"3\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D447 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> is normal. Justify your\nanswer.</li>\n<li>Which hypothesis of <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"thm-simultaneous-unitary-diagonalization\">@thm-simultaneous-unitary-diagonalization</span>\nis dropped in <span class=\"citation\" data-cites=\"thm-simultaneous-unitary-triangularization\">@thm-simultaneous-unitary-triangularization</span>,\nand what is lost in the conclusion?</li>\n</ol>\n</div>","file":"ch12-spectral-theory/07-commuting-normal-families.html"}